WEBVTT

00:00:00.738 --> 00:00:04.302
<v><b>Continuity announcer:</b></v> The Food Chain is a BBC World Service podcast series

00:00:04.357 --> 00:00:10.011
which focuses on the business, science, and cultural significance of food in our lives.

00:00:10.154 --> 00:00:13.260
This episode, Love you, hate your food,

00:00:13.427 --> 00:00:17.209
focuses on two couples with very different tastes in food,

00:00:17.447 --> 00:00:20.855
and the effect it has had on their lives and relationships.

00:00:21.386 --> 00:00:24.509
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> Meals were a battlefield for Sheryl and Dharmesh.

00:00:24.834 --> 00:00:28.205
She grew up on southern American food in Tennessee,

00:00:28.475 --> 00:00:31.986
but he preferred the typical Indian cooking in his family.

00:00:32.406 --> 00:00:37.213
Rebecca and Saj are a London couple dealing with a major split over food –

00:00:37.546 --> 00:00:42.734
she’s a vegan, he eats meat. Both couples talk to Emily Thomas.

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:47.400
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> Sheryl and Dharmesh Pabu are in their late forties and live in Atlanta,

00:00:47.399 --> 00:00:52.872
Georgia, in the US. His family is originally from Gujarat in western India.

00:00:53.277 --> 00:00:55.519
She was raised in Tennessee.

00:00:55.741 --> 00:00:57.178
<v><b>Sheryl:</b></v> I’m a very Southern girl,

00:00:57.178 --> 00:01:00.575
so the kinds of things that I loved to eat were fried chicken,

00:01:00.574 --> 00:01:06.515
pot roasts with potatoes, you know, seasoned with salt and pepper, nothing spicy.

00:01:06.746 --> 00:01:11.457
I ate a lot of hamburgers and hotdogs, sort of the all-American type of food.

00:01:11.766 --> 00:01:13.707
<v><b>Dharmesh:</b></v> We were raised vegetarian,

00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:18.788
so lots of rice and vegetables and carbs and lots of spices.

00:01:19.169 --> 00:01:24.678
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> And Sheryl, when did you first realize that you had such different attitudes to food?

00:01:24.678 --> 00:01:28.231
<v><b>Sheryl:</b></v> It was when his mother first had me over for dinner to their house

00:01:28.381 --> 00:01:33.671
and fixed a vegetable biriyani with this yogurt motald sauce that goes on it.

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:37.008
There was no meat and it was very spicy.

00:01:37.175 --> 00:01:41.125
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> Dharmesh was training to be a dentist whilst Sheryl stayed at home,

00:01:41.228 --> 00:01:43.062
so she did all the cooking.

00:01:43.213 --> 00:01:45.567
<v><b>Sheryl:</b></v> My simple, you know, chicken and potatoes,

00:01:45.592 --> 00:01:47.916
basic foods, and – that was interesting,

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:52.905
because he would put masala and anything hot and spicy on top of it that he could find.

00:01:53.239 --> 00:01:56.769
I felt a little bit insulted, like, you know,

00:01:56.768 --> 00:02:00.239
you can understand cultural differences in your head,

00:02:00.239 --> 00:02:02.385
but in your heart it’s, it’s hard to accept.

00:02:02.385 --> 00:02:05.235
I felt, like, ‘Wow, he doesn’t like my cooking!’

00:02:05.505 --> 00:02:08.473
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> And, Dharmesh, what were you thinking at the time?

00:02:08.472 --> 00:02:11.981
Were you enjoying this food that Sheryl was making? Be honest.

00:02:12.791 --> 00:02:16.200
<v><b>Dharmesh:</b></v> Er, it was very bland, honestly.

00:02:16.572 --> 00:02:19.377
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> But Dharmesh’s mum lived nearby

00:02:19.377 --> 00:02:23.159
and she’d regularly turn up at the house with meals.

00:02:23.429 --> 00:02:25.504
<v><b>Dharmesh:</b></v> She brought food any time she could,

00:02:25.742 --> 00:02:29.714
and sometimes it was a bit of a tug of war with Mom and Sheryl

00:02:29.714 --> 00:02:32.067
because they’d both have food in front of me

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.722
and watch which one I’m gonna eat first, so…

00:02:36.792 --> 00:02:39.076
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> I mean, that must have been quite difficult for you

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:40.790
because you wouldn’t want to offend either.

00:02:41.139 --> 00:02:43.123
<v><b>Dharmesh:</b></v> Yes, so I overate a lot.

00:02:45.596 --> 00:02:48.508
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> Dharmesh, thinking back to those early years of marriage

00:02:48.508 --> 00:02:50.467
with your mum bringing all this food around,

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:52.746
what do you think was going on for her?

00:02:52.747 --> 00:02:55.500
Why do you think she felt she needed to come over and feed you?

00:02:56.147 --> 00:02:58.737
<v><b>Dharmesh:</b></v> I think that’s just the way Mom shows her love.

00:02:58.888 --> 00:03:02.518
She cooks endless amounts of food for everybody.

00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:07.611
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> Do you think that your conflicts over food were about the food itself,

00:03:07.611 --> 00:03:09.646
or was it about what the food represented?

00:03:09.821 --> 00:03:14.357
<v><b>Sheryl:</b></v> Oh, definitely about what the food represented, 100 per cent,

00:03:14.357 --> 00:03:18.299
because over the years I have grown to love Indian food,

00:03:18.566 --> 00:03:21.801
but it definitely was a symbol of, you know,

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:25.862
the relationship that I had maybe with his mother and, sort of,

00:03:25.862 --> 00:03:31.031
Indian culture as a whole, because I wasn’t really accepted for so many years.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.266
When we would get together with family – you know, extended family –

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:36.889
for dinners, the women would be in the kitchen cooking

00:03:37.046 --> 00:03:40.305
and the men would be sitting, you know, in front of the TV.

00:03:40.463 --> 00:03:43.864
And I didn’t want to be in the kitchen in that role

00:03:44.197 --> 00:03:48.916
because I didn’t want anyone to pressure me to fix a certain type of food,

00:03:49.274 --> 00:03:54.216
because I am a meat and potatoes kind of girl, and that’s OK, too.

00:03:54.581 --> 00:03:57.896
When we eat together now, he’ll eat something different,

00:03:57.896 --> 00:04:00.494
I’ll eat something different. Or I’ll make a dish –

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:03.002
like, we had Fettuccine Alfredo last night,

00:04:03.002 --> 00:04:07.238
and I had mine with some Parmesan cheese and he had his with some masala,

00:04:07.429 --> 00:04:08.861
and I just say ‘Whatever’.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:12.204
<v><b>Dharmesh:</b></v> I think we have more of a blended menu now,

00:04:12.679 --> 00:04:14.659
just happy to be in a kitchen together,

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:17.058
cooking together and having family time.

00:04:18.027 --> 00:04:21.818
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> And does your mum, Dharmesh, still bring you food?

00:04:22.032 --> 00:04:23.295
<v><b>Dharmesh:</b></v> Yes, absolutely.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.618
<v><b>Emily:</b></v> How often does she bring you food?

00:04:26.967 --> 00:04:29.033
<v><b>Dharmesh:</b></v> She’d bring it daily, if I allowed it,

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:31.686
but we set some boundaries and, you know…

00:04:31.686 --> 00:04:32.929
maybe a few times a week.